It is known from prior research that during L-PBF (Ref  33 ) and other rapid solidification processes (Ref  34 ,  35 ) D2 tool steel solidifies mainly as austenitic. A lower carbon content (1,09 wt.% C) Cr-C steel has been reported to solidify fully austenitic during laser processing (Ref  36 ), indicating that in D2 steel the extra carbon is driven into the eutectic formed by austenite and M7C3. Based on XRD curves in Fig.  4 , the as-manufactured sample is in line with prior observations of austenitic structure with slight indications of M7C3 carbides, which represents the eutectic solidification microstructure of the D2 steel (Ref  37 ). It has been reported that the interdendritic region consisting of austenite and M7C3 can be prone to hot cracking during direct energy deposition (Ref  38 ), but in the present research we did not observe any hot cracks. The sample heat-treated at 550 °C remains mostly austenitic, whereas annealing at 600 and 650 °C result in transformation into ferritic or martensitic structure, accompanied by M7C3 carbides as indicated by the small peaks between 45 and 65° 2 θ . The small peaks in all conditions are concluded to be M7C3 based on (Ref  39 ,  40 ) and, in the context of D2 steel, the result is supported by SEM observations in (Ref  34 ,  38 ), XRD in (Ref  35 ,  38 ) and TEM in (Ref  31 ). Furthermore, the data was indexed using ICDD database and M7C3 carbides provided the best fit. Some carbide peaks are present in all samples becoming more profound together with the alpha peaks as the annealing temperature is increased. The indexed peak positions and peak parameters are presented in Appendix A. The lattice parameters were calculated from interplanar spacings with Eq  1  and are shown in Table  2 . Phase fractions from Rietveld refinement are presented in Table  3 . A Thermo-Calc calculation (TC2025b, TCFE13) showed maximum M7C3 equilibrium mass fraction at 650 °C being 17.8% indicating that the samples have not reached equilibr